{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1550217-essence-how-to-interpret-the-rules-for-feats-of-strength","id":1550217,"title":"Essence: how to interpret the rules for feats of strength","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-04-17T10:52:31-07:00","id":1550217,"title":"Essence: how to interpret the rules for feats of strength","content":"I'm trying to figure out what kind of objects can be readily destroyed via World-Subsuming Omphalos Stance in the new Player's Guide preview. It breaks objects &quot;<span style=\"font-family:gg sans\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">as if by an extreme feat of strength with 10 successes</span></span>.&quot;<br /> <br /> Essence page 150 says an &quot;extreme&quot; feat of strength is difficulty 7. But it also divides the feat examples into &quot;standard,&quot; &quot;challenging,&quot; and &quot;legendary&quot; and I can't find any definitions for those terms (in the venture rules or otherwise). I have no idea what the intent here is; am I missing something?<br /> <br /> (Of course, in practice the GM always decides, but surely the listed guidelines are intended to have <i>some</i> meaning.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11237-jefepato","name":"Jefepato","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11237_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"557"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-17T12:34:11-07:00","id":1550224,"title":"","content":"I think that's just a versioning error; I'd assume standard -&gt; lesser, challenging -&gt; greater, legendary -&gt; extreme.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/396-mr-rage","name":"Mr Rage","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"14"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-17T13:48:48-07:00","id":1550227,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mr Rage</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1550217-essence-how-to-interpret-the-rules-for-feats-of-strength?p=1550224#post1550224\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that's just a versioning error; I'd assume standard -&gt; lesser, challenging -&gt; greater, legendary -&gt; extreme.</div> </div> </div> </div>That interpretation seems hard to square with how Essence p. 150 presents things. Each of the &quot;lesser,&quot; &quot;greater,&quot; and &quot;extreme&quot; paragraphs have their own individual examples of &quot;standard&quot; and &quot;challenging&quot; feats, with the &quot;extreme&quot; paragraph including &quot;legendary&quot; feats as well. Lifting a warhorse is lesser/challenging, lifting a yeddim is greater/standard.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11237-jefepato","name":"Jefepato","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11237_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"557"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-28T04:04:33-07:00","id":1551324,"title":"","content":"Looking at the text, my take is that what you're trying to life or break determines whether it's a <b>lesser</b>, <b>greater</b>, or <b>extreme </b>feat of strength, and then the exact size within each category gives you the difficulty, but you need the listed dice pool to even attempt the feat in the first place. Assuming that you're using your best Attribute, then <b>Lesser</b> feats of strength don't require any dots in Physique (and the text says that lesser feats of strength can be performed without a roll if you have at least 1 dot), <b>Greater</b> feats require up to 5 dots (depending on how you read the &quot;5-9&quot;; does the Storyteller assign the exact pool requirement depending on how big the target is?), and <b>Extreme</b> feats of strength require a pool of 10 dice; which I <i>think</i> means that a starting character with 5 dots in Physique would have to spend a stunt or activate the Excellency to even attempt it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28792-beron-the-grey","name":"Beron the Grey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28792_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}